 "text": "The clause is likely **enforceable**. Under the RTA, a tenancy agreement term is void only to the extent it contravenes the Act. Section 14(1) makes provisions that purport to alter the Act's effect void, and this clause does not do that. Nothing in the Act prohibits a landlord from requiring dogs to be leashed in shared areas or tenants to clean up after their animals there. Those are reasonable terms for the use of common property, and they don't limit any right the Act gives the tenant, such as the right to reasonable enjoyment of the unit under s. 22. The clause would become problematic only if it restricted the tenant's use of the rental unit itself in a way the Act forbids, or if it were applied in a way that amounted to harassment or interference.\n\nI'm working from memory of the Act's section numbering and wording, so check s. 14 and the current text on the Ontario e-Laws site before relying on this.\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 14",
